<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muscovy Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1555</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Russia Company</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Muscovy Company was an English trading company chartered in 1555 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-chancellor">Richard Chancellor</a>'s voyage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a> opened direct contact with the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>. One of the first major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joint-stock-company">joint-stock companies</a>, it held a monopoly of English trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, enjoyed privileges there, and sponsored voyages in search of a north-east passage and whaling at Spitsbergen. It lost its Russian privileges in 1649.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muscovy-company.jpg" alt="Muscovy Company" width="215" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuscovyCompany_Seal_1555.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
